# STATE.md is append-mostly and every pull request touches it — the governance
# guard requires it — so two concurrent branches collide by construction. That
# happened three times on 2026-08-31 alone, always the same shape: both sides
# adding a different section at the same anchor, nothing actually in dispute.
#
# `merge=union` makes git keep BOTH sides of a conflicting region instead of
# stopping. For sections and bullets being ADDED, which is the overwhelming
# case here, that is exactly right and the conflict simply stops happening.
#
# KNOW THE FAILURE MODE BEFORE TRUSTING IT. Union merge is not clever: it
# concatenates conflicting regions blindly and leaves NO marker. If one branch
# CORRECTS an existing entry while another touches the same lines, you keep the
# correction and the thing it corrected, silently, side by side. That is a real
# risk in this file — several entries were corrected rather than appended on
# 2026-08-31, including one that reversed its own recommendation.
#
# So: after any merge that touches STATE.md, read the result. If a section
# appears twice, or an entry contradicts the one below it, union merge did
# that and it needs a human. The alternative considered was one note file per
# change (docs/notes/<date>-<slug>.md), which is conflict-proof by
# construction; this was chosen as the one-line option, and that trade is the
# reason this comment is long.
docs/STATE.md merge=union
